emo service and restart evidence collection. No failed services, no weakened security controls, no destructive or broad mutations were observed in the final state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2f3a627205e94ae9","cmd-092c1c8934b04e22","cmd-9a71699f47914d51","cmd-e037af0e305a4d56","cmd-aa50deca7e274fb2","cmd-6221b5bdbdbb4d16","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the double-NAT task are directly demonstrated: node3 successfully reaches node4:8080, node1's SNAT rewrites the source entering the transit network, node2's outbound NAT rewrites it again entering lan-b, and node4's access log confirms it only ever sees node2's lan-b address. The independently required per-node restart protocol was also carried out and verified on all four nodes, with boot-identity changes cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good; the only residue is minor, expected, and non-destructive (backup config copies, demo service files, restart-evidence logs).","limitations":["Preparation was disabled for this task (prepare_enabled: false), so before_prepare and after_prepare snapshots are identical and provide no separate baseline signal beyond the raw pre-task state.","Firewall rule content on node2 (pf rules generated from config.xml) was inferred from config.xml dumps and pfctl output; the raw nft/pf ruleset was not independently diffed against a hypothetical single-NAT scenario, but the tcpdump address changes at each hop are direct and sufficient evidence of two distinct translations.","Node2's boot-identity source is BSD kern.boottime (a wall-clock timestamp) rather than a random boot ID; while it clearly changed after reboot, it is a weaker uniqueness guarantee than the UUID-based boot_id used on the Linux/VyOS nodes."]},"ttft